 The sign-in and sign-up forms submit credentials to the same-origin `/api/auth` route. Configure an asymmetric JWT signing key (RSA or ECDSA) in Supabase Auth so `auth.getClaims()` can verify claims using the cached JWKS rather than making an Auth network request. Supabase's `getClaims()` may still use the Auth server for projects using a symmetric signing key, so local verification—and its latency benefit—depends on the signing-key algorithm. Middleware refreshes sessions and verifies claims on protected dashboard routes, then forwards a minimal, server-only verified identity header; dashboard server components consume that header rather than verifying the same token again. Keep remote `getUser()` checks for operations that need a current, authoritative user record.[m
